llvm-4.0.0 backport patch: the anti-dependency breaker skips a def of a register it must keep (PS4)

This patch CHANGES CODE GENERATION after register allocation, where the
post-RA scheduler's critical anti-dependency breaker (btver2 enables that
scheduler) renames a register. r270935 (Mitch Bodart, 2016-05-26,
"[CodeGen] Fix problem with X86 byte registers exposed by r268845",
llvm-project 05aeeb5cf19e, first release LLVM 3.9) made
CriticalAntiDepBreaker::ScanInstruction process a def of a register already
in KeepRegs (its def index, kill index and class reset, the register kept)
where earlier releases skipped such a def. With it the pc load of
0xdb00e0 and 0xdafdc0 is renamed to esi; the prebuilt 3.8.1 and the base
rename it to r8d and edi (round 194B: the scheduler's input is identical;
`-break-anti-dependencies=none` and `=all` both stay unequal; no source form
reaches a rename made after allocation). With the hunk reversed exactly
those two functions compile to the base's bytes and they are the only two
of the module's 4,679 functions that change; no matched game row changes
(round 196B's module sweep).

diff -ru a/lib/CodeGen/CriticalAntiDepBreaker.cpp b/lib/CodeGen/CriticalAntiDepBreaker.cpp
--- a/lib/CodeGen/CriticalAntiDepBreaker.cpp
+++ b/lib/CodeGen/CriticalAntiDepBreaker.cpp
@@ -260,24 +260,25 @@
       if (Reg == 0) continue;
       if (!MO.isDef()) continue;
 
+      // PS4: a def of a register already in KeepRegs is skipped, as before
+      // r270935; its Keep flag and the def bookkeeping of a kept register are
+      // not taken.
+      // If we've already marked this reg as unchangeable, carry on.
+      if (KeepRegs.test(Reg)) continue;
+
       // Ignore two-addr defs.
       if (MI.isRegTiedToUseOperand(i))
         continue;
 
-      // If we've already marked this reg as unchangeable, don't remove
-      // it or any of its subregs from KeepRegs.
-      bool Keep = KeepRegs.test(Reg);
-
       // For the reg itself and all subregs: update the def to current;
       // reset the kill state, any restrictions, and references.
       for (MCSubRegIterator SRI(Reg, TRI, true); SRI.isValid(); ++SRI) {
         unsigned SubregReg = *SRI;
         DefIndices[SubregReg] = Count;
         KillIndices[SubregReg] = ~0u;
+        KeepRegs.reset(SubregReg);
         Classes[SubregReg] = nullptr;
         RegRefs.erase(SubregReg);
-        if (!Keep)
-          KeepRegs.reset(SubregReg);
       }
       // Conservatively mark super-registers as unusable.
       for (MCSuperRegIterator SR(Reg, TRI); SR.isValid(); ++SR)
